from docbuilder import *

blocks = []
A = blocks.append

A(H1("Chapter 18 - Difficult Teacher Questions and Answers"))
A(P("More than 120 questions, grouped by topic. Answers are written to be spoken: 2-6 sentences, correct to the code. Questions marked **[M]** are the mandatory ones from your list. "
    "If a question goes beyond what is written here, say honestly what ANVESH does and what it does not - the panel respects \"not established\" far more than a confident guess."))

A(H2("18.1 The project as a whole"))
A(QA([
    ("Q1 [M] Explain the project in one minute.", "ANVESH turns a fixed CCTV video into reviewable road-event candidates. It detects and tracks vehicles with YOLOv8n and ByteTrack, converts positions to metres with a homography, and raises candidates such as stopped vehicles, congestion, over-speed, wrong-way and possible collision. "
        "Each candidate becomes an incident with hashed evidence, and an officer-led case workflow with four-eyes review ends in a *sandbox* challan reference. It is research and demo only: plate OCR, a real registry and a real challan system are not connected."),
    ("Q2 [M] What is the novelty of ANVESH?", "The individual algorithms - YOLO, ByteTrack, homography, Dempster-Shafer, kinematic waves - are all standard and the code says so. The contribution is the **integration with honesty built in**: no km/h without a validated calibration, a cause ranking that may abstain, candidates instead of verdicts, a case state machine with separation of duties, and tamper-evident evidence and audit. I would not claim a new algorithm."),
    ("Q3 What problem does it solve that CCTV alone does not?", "CCTV records; it does not notify, summarise or prove. ANVESH finds the event, jumps to the moment, saves original frames and an annotated clip with hashes, and records who reviewed what. That saves search time and makes the evidence defensible."),
    ("Q4 Who are the users?", "Investigators (control room), reviewers (independent verifiers), officers (approvers) and administrators. Each role has different permissions so no single person can run the whole chain."),
    ("Q5 What does ANVESH stand for?", "Not established in the current implementation. The repository defines no acronym; the product tagline is \"Road event investigation\". I would not invent an expansion."),
    ("Q6 Is this a finished product?", "No. It is a verified research/demo system: the tests pass and a 20-minute run works, but accuracy on real cameras is unmeasured, plate OCR, registry, e-challan and MFA are missing, and no real CCTV hardware test was done."),
    ("Q7 What is real and what is simulated in your demo?", "Real: detection, tracking, counts, approximate speed, stopped-vehicle and congestion incidents with evidence, the case workflow and audit. Simulated: the wrong-way, collision and queue scenarios (scripted boxes, labelled SIMULATED) and every registry and challan result (sandbox)."),
    ("Q8 Is the demo video processed live? [M]", "It is a recorded file replayed at real time: the reader releases frame *i* at time i/25 s, and detection, tracking, events and evidence are computed while it plays. Nothing is precomputed or read from a result file. It is not a live camera feed; RTSP support exists in code but was only tested with fake captures."),
    ("Q9 Why a fixed camera?", "Homography, lane polygons, zones and the background-based calibration assume the camera does not move. A moving camera would need continuous re-calibration."),
    ("Q10 What is the difference between monitoring, intelligence and investigation?", "Monitoring shows what is happening; intelligence summarises the traffic state over time; investigation explains an event and preserves proof. ANVESH does all three, with investigation as its focus."),
]))

A(H2("18.2 Detection and YOLO"))
A(QA([
    ("Q11 [M] Why did you choose YOLO?", "YOLO detects all objects in one network pass, so it is fast enough for 25 fps on a laptop GPU, it is widely documented, and Ultralytics offers ByteTrack integrated with it. The measured detect+track time is about 25 ms per frame."),
    ("Q12 [M] Why YOLOv8n rather than a larger model?", "Speed and headroom. The nano model has about 3.2 million parameters and leaves GPU time for tracking, encoding and the dashboard. A larger model might detect small far vehicles better, but we have no labelled footage to measure that, and it could break real-time."),
    ("Q13 [M] What does the n stand for?", "Nano - the smallest of the YOLOv8 sizes n, s, m, l and x."),
    ("Q14 What does the model take as input and give as output?", "Input: one image (640 x 360 here, padded/resized by Ultralytics). Output after filtering: for each vehicle a box (x1, y1, x2, y2), a class and a confidence score."),
    ("Q15 [M] What is IoU?", "Intersection over Union: overlap area divided by combined area of two boxes, from 0 (no overlap) to 1 (identical). For two 10 x 8 boxes overlapping 5 x 5 it is 25 / 135 = 0.185. It is used in NMS, in ByteTrack matching and in scoring detectors."),
    ("Q16 [M] What is NMS?", "Non-Maximum Suppression. The network proposes many overlapping boxes for one vehicle; NMS keeps the highest-scoring one and removes others whose IoU with it exceeds 0.7 (Ultralytics default)."),
    ("Q17 What is the confidence threshold and why 0.25?", "Boxes with confidence below 0.25 are dropped. 0.25 is the Ultralytics predict default and ANVESH passes it explicitly. It trades missed vehicles against false boxes; we did not tune it on labelled data."),
    ("Q18 What do precision, recall and mAP mean?", "Precision: of the boxes drawn, how many were real vehicles. Recall: of the real vehicles, how many were found. mAP@50 averages precision over recall when a box counts as correct at IoU 0.50; mAP@50-95 repeats this over stricter IoUs."),
    ("Q19 How does the model tell car, bus and truck apart?", "The network outputs a score per class and the highest is chosen. The COCO model has 80 classes; ANVESH keeps only bicycle, car, motorcycle, bus and truck, mapped by name. Lorries are sometimes labelled bus, and a track's class can flicker."),
    ("Q20 Which checkpoint is the default and which is fine-tuned?", "`models/yolov8n.pt` (COCO-pretrained) is the default. `models/finetuned/anvesh_mio5_yolov8n_best.pt`, fine-tuned on MIO-TCD, is opt-in only."),
    ("Q21 Why was the fine-tuned model not promoted although its MIO-TCD score is much higher?", "mAP@50 0.215 -> 0.870 is on MIO-TCD test images with MIO-TCD's class definitions. On real CCTV (CityFlow) recall was 0.718 versus 0.748, so no improvement. We have no labels for the demo footage, and a 300 s run changed incident counts without any way to say which is correct. So promotion would be a claim without evidence."),
    ("Q22 Did the fine-tuned model do better on your demonstration video?", "On unlabelled proxies it gave fewer, longer tracks (615 vs 808 IDs, median 2.84 s vs 1.96 s) and higher mean confidence. These are proxies - fewer IDs could also mean wrongly merged vehicles - so I do not claim better accuracy."),
    ("Q23 What dataset did you train on, and what are the limits?", "MIO-TCD (88,270 train / 10,865 val / 10,865 test images), 8 epochs, not converged. It is CC BY-NC-SA 4.0, so the weights are for non-commercial research. It is North American traffic; it says nothing about Indian traffic, night or rain."),
    ("Q24 Why can it not detect auto-rickshaws?", "COCO has no such class and no ANVESH checkpoint was trained with one. The taxonomy includes `auto_rickshaw`, but it would only be produced by a model trained for it."),
    ("Q25 How accurate is the detector on your video?", "Not established. There are no labels for demo.mp4. The honest statement is that detections look plausible and tracks are stable enough to build events, but accuracy is unmeasured; labelling about 200 frames is the first next step."),
    ("Q26 What happens to the frame before YOLO sees it?", "Ultralytics resizes and pads it to the network size (default 640, a multiple of 32), converts it to a tensor and normalises it. ANVESH does no extra preprocessing on the video frame."),
    ("Q27 Why not use a transformer detector?", "No labelled data to justify it, higher cost per frame, and YOLOv8n already meets the real-time target. It would be an option once accuracy is measured."),
    ("Q28 Is the model licence a problem?", "Yes, an open item: Ultralytics is AGPL-3.0 (or a paid enterprise licence), and the fine-tuned weights come from NonCommercial data. A real deployment needs a legal decision or a permissively licensed detector."),
]))

A(H2("18.3 Tracking and IDs"))
A(QA([
    ("Q29 [M] How are vehicle IDs allocated?", "In two layers. ByteTrack (Ultralytics) gives a raw integer ID from a counter that starts at 1 and only increases; ANVESH's TrackManager then maps it to a session identity (camera, session, serial) with the serial starting at 0. The dashboard shows the serial, e.g. 00051."),
    ("Q30 [M] How many IDs can be allocated?", "No fixed limit in the code - Python integers are unbounded. The practical limit comes from ByteTrack emitting IDs in a float32 array, exact up to 2^24 = 16.7 million; at about 4.6 IDs per second that is roughly six weeks of one continuous session. That is derived from the library, not tested."),
    ("Q31 [M] Can IDs be reused?", "Not within a tracker run: the counter only grows. They restart from 1 when a new tracker is created, which is why ANVESH adds its own session identity with a random per-session run id so two sessions cannot collide."),
    ("Q32 [M] What happens when a vehicle disappears?", "ByteTrack marks the track Lost and keeps predicting its position with the Kalman filter for up to 30 frames (1.2 s at 25 fps). If a box overlaps the prediction in that time the same ID resumes; after 30 frames the track is removed."),
    ("Q33 If it comes back later, is it the same vehicle?", "ANVESH cannot tell. It has no appearance or plate re-identification, so a returning vehicle gets a new ID and a new tracklet. Incidents say 'same physical vehicle after a tracker break is not established'."),
    ("Q34 [M] Does ByteTrack use a Kalman filter?", "Yes. Ultralytics' `KalmanFilterXYAH` keeps an 8-number state (x, y, aspect ratio, height and their velocities) with a constant-velocity model. It predicts each track's box before matching and is corrected by the matched detection."),
    ("Q35 [M] How does ByteTrack associate detections?", "Stage 1 matches existing tracks to high-score boxes (>= 0.25) by cost 1 - IoU x score with a one-to-one assignment (`lap.lapjv`), accepting cost <= 0.8. Stage 2 matches the still-unmatched tracks to low-score boxes (0.1-0.25) by IoU >= 0.5. Leftover high boxes with score >= 0.25 start new tracks."),
    ("Q36 [M] Why are low-confidence detections useful?", "A vehicle that is partly hidden or blurred scores low for a few frames. If a low box overlaps an existing track it is probably that vehicle, so keeping it avoids a track break. A low box that overlaps nothing is ignored, so it does not create false tracks."),
    ("Q37 In your system are low-confidence boxes actually used?", "Normally not. ANVESH passes conf=0.25 to `track()`, so boxes below 0.25 are removed before ByteTrack sees them and stage 2 gets no input. We found this in Session 7 and tested conf 0.10; it is available as an option but not adopted."),
    ("Q38 [M] Why were the tracking settings not changed?", "conf 0.10 cut IDs by 8 % but added about 10 % more boxes and pushed the congestion proxy over its threshold for flowing traffic. match_thresh 0.9 cut IDs most (808 -> 660) but is a looser matcher and we cannot prove, without labels, that different vehicles are not being joined. A change we cannot validate is not safer than the default."),
    ("Q39 What is a track state?", "New (just created), Tracked (matched), Lost (unmatched but kept alive), Removed. A track is confirmed when it is matched a second time, and only confirmed tracks are output."),
    ("Q40 Why can IDs fragment?", "Small far-field boxes flicker in and out, vehicles are hidden behind lorries, the detector's class or box jitters, and a gap of more than 30 frames removes the track. In the demo the median box is only 28 px wide."),
    ("Q41 Why can two vehicles be confused?", "ByteTrack uses only box position, size and score, no appearance. When two vehicles cross or overlap, the box that matches the predicted position can swap, causing an ID switch."),
    ("Q42 [M] Why did the 20-minute demo create 5,552 IDs?", "Those are tracklets, not vehicles: 808 IDs in 180 s scales to about 5,390 in 1,200 s, and the measured value is 5,552 (4,339 car, 714 truck, 499 bus). The dashboard text says 'session-scoped identities, not unique vehicles'."),
    ("Q43 What is the difference between a track ID and a vehicle identity?", "A track ID names one uninterrupted tracker run. A vehicle identity (this exact car, its plate, its owner) would need re-identification and plate reading, which ANVESH does not do automatically."),
    ("Q44 Are IDs shared across cameras?", "No. The identity key includes the camera id, but nothing links cameras."),
    ("Q45 What happens to IDs when the server restarts?", "A new session uses a new random run id and the raw counter restarts. Stored incidents keep their old track-id text; nothing is reconciled between runs."),
    ("Q46 Could two tracks share an ID by mistake?", "Inside one tracker, no. ANVESH's TrackManager keeps the invariant that an identity key never refers to two tracklets and counts anomalies such as the same raw ID twice in a frame. One theoretical risk: Ultralytics' counter is class-global, so two ByteTrack trackers in one process could disturb each other - not an issue in the demo, noted as a limitation."),
    ("Q47 Why did you use ByteTrack and not DeepSORT?", "The blueprint chose the simplest tracker that is good enough for V1, and ByteTrack ships with Ultralytics. DeepSORT-type trackers add an appearance network, which costs GPU time; we had no data to show a benefit."),
]))

A(H2("18.4 FPS, video and real-time performance"))
A(QA([
    ("Q48 [M] How many frames are processed per second?", "About 25: the average over the 20-minute run was 24.89 FPS for a 25 fps source, with 30,001 frames processed and none dropped. The video is paced to real time, so this shows it kept up, not its maximum speed."),
    ("Q49 [M] How do you calculate FPS?", "FPS = processed frames / processing time. 30,001 frames in 1,200.66 s is 24.99; the monitor's 24.89 divides by a slightly longer time because the clock starts at session creation, including model load."),
    ("Q50 What is the frame interval at 24.89 FPS?", "1 / 24.89 = 0.0402 s, about 40.2 ms (40.0 ms at exactly 25 fps)."),
    ("Q51 What is latency and how is it measured?", "Latency is completion time minus start time. ANVESH records `frame_processing` (start to end of `process_frame`) and `frame_end_to_end` (from the frame's release by the source, so including queue waiting). Median about 27-28 ms."),
    ("Q52 Why is p95 latency not the same as FPS?", "FPS is a long-run average of throughput; p95 latency is the time that 95 % of single frames stay under. p95 = 38.9 ms is still under the 40.2 ms frame interval, so the system keeps up even for slow frames."),
    ("Q53 What is the difference between FPS and the real-time factor?", "FPS counts frames per wall-clock second; real-time factor is video seconds per wall second (1200.0 / 1200.66 = 0.9995). For a 25 fps video RTF = FPS / 25."),
    ("Q54 What does the 20-minute test prove and not prove?", "It shows incremental, bounded-memory processing that kept up with a 25 fps source (0 dropped, memory +1.8 %). It does not prove accuracy, higher throughput, long-term stability or behaviour on live cameras."),
    ("Q55 [M] What happens if FPS falls?", "For a recorded file the queue is BLOCK: the reader waits, no frame is lost and the video simply plays slower. For a live source the queue is DROP_OLDEST: the oldest frames are discarded and counted, affected windows are marked degraded and the dashboard shows dropped frames and queue depth."),
    ("Q56 What is a bounded queue and why does it matter?", "A queue with a maximum size (8 frames). Without a bound a slow worker would let frames pile up until memory is exhausted. With a bound the system either waits or drops - both visible and counted."),
    ("Q57 How do you avoid preprocessing the whole video?", "The reader yields one frame at a time and the engine processes it immediately; windows, track history and the frame ring are trimmed by time, so memory does not grow with video length."),
    ("Q58 How is the GPU used?", "Ultralytics runs the network on CUDA automatically when available; ANVESH sets no device. The dashboard shows a GPU chip from `torch.cuda.is_available()`. Utilisation was at most 64 %. The `nvidia-smi` memory figure looked implausibly low and was not cross-checked."),
    ("Q59 What is GPU warm-up?", "The first inference includes CUDA context creation and kernel selection, so the first frame is slow. The largest frame time in the run was 2,187 ms, which the reports attribute to this, but it was not confirmed."),
    ("Q60 Could it run faster than real time?", "Probably: frame processing averaged 27.8 ms, so about 36 FPS if unpaced (derived; the paced run did not measure the ceiling). `run_demo.py --fast` processes as fast as possible, but it is for testing only."),
    ("Q61 Why 10-second windows?", "A policy choice (`window_s` = 10): long enough to count vehicles and dwell, short enough to react. Windows tile the timeline without overlap because the congestion state machine needs consecutive windows."),
]))

A(H2("18.5 Geometry, calibration and speed"))
A(QA([
    ("Q62 [M] What is homography?", "A 3 x 3 matrix that maps points of one plane to another - here the image to the flat road. With a 4-point minimum it is solved from known correspondences, and it handles perspective, where equal road lengths take fewer pixels far away."),
    ("Q63 [M] Why do we need camera calibration?", "Pixels have no fixed length. Calibration supplies reference points with known road coordinates so that a pixel can be converted to metres; without it speed in km/h would be a guess."),
    ("Q64 How did you calibrate this camera?", "From lane markings: motorway dashes of 2 m with 7 m gaps (UK diagram 1005.1) and a 3.65 m lane width gave 10 reference points; `cv2.findHomography` fitted H (RMSE 0.094 m); dash 3 was held out (RMSE 0.43 m)."),
    ("Q65 What does RMSE 0.094 m mean?", "The fitted homography reproduces the 10 reference points to a typical error of 9.4 cm - an in-sample number, so by itself it proves little."),
    ("Q66 What does the hold-out RMSE of 0.43 m show?", "That points not used in the fit also land within 43 cm of where the standard says they should be - geometric self-consistency. It does not verify the assumed 9 m pitch because a homography can absorb any uniform scale."),
    ("Q67 [M] How do you calculate speed?", "Convert each ground point to metres with H, fit a straight line through the last 1.6 s of positions by least squares, and take the slope in m/s times 3.6 for km/h. Speed (km/h) = distance (m) / time (s) x 3.6."),
    ("Q68 [M] Why is speed approximate?", "The scale rests on unverified assumptions (the road follows that marking standard, 3.65 m lanes, planar road, unaltered video), and the displayed +/- band covers only measurement noise, not that scale uncertainty, which could be 10 % or more."),
    ("Q69 Why regression instead of distance over time between two frames?", "One pixel of box jitter over 0.04 s changes the speed by ten percent or more (our example gave 87 km/h for a true 80 km/h). Fitting a line to all samples averages the noise out."),
    ("Q70 Why the bottom centre of the box?", "That point is closest to where the vehicle touches the road, which is the plane the homography describes. The box centre of a tall lorry floats above the road and would give wrong positions."),
    ("Q71 Why is speed shown only in the magenta zone?", "The calibration is trustworthy only in the near field of the toward-camera carriageway (rows >= 225). Outside it the scale is extrapolated and noisy."),
    ("Q72 What if the calibration is invalid or absent?", "Then no speed number is produced at all (`no_metric_calibration`); motion stays in pixels. This 'speed-safety invariant' is enforced in the data types."),
    ("Q73 Why does a vehicle look smaller as it moves away?", "Perspective projection: image size falls roughly with distance. The road plane compresses the same way, which is why the pixel-to-metre scale changes from 0.07 m/px at the bottom row to 0.42 m/px at row 225."),
    ("Q74 How would you improve speed accuracy?", "Survey two known distances on the actual road, replace the marking-standard validation with a survey hold-out, calibrate a camera at a good height/angle, keep the camera fixed, and tune thresholds on labelled examples."),
    ("Q75 How are lanes defined?", "As hand-drawn polygons in image pixels in the camera configuration, and a vehicle's lane is the polygon containing its ground point. They are not detected automatically."),
]))

A(H2("18.6 Events and violations"))
A(QA([
    ("Q76 [M] How do you detect a stopped vehicle?", "A track's ground points must stay within a 0.75 m radius of the mean of the last 3 s, and the run must last at least 3 s (dwell). That is equivalent to a speed below about 0.5 m/s. It uses episodes that span window boundaries so a stop is not missed or double counted."),
    ("Q77 What is a long-duration stop?", "A stopped-vehicle episode that lasts at least 30 s (`long_stop_s`) is escalated to `long_duration_stop`; outside a queue it is rated high."),
    ("Q78 [M] How do you distinguish a traffic queue from an obstruction?", "Two signals: the camera's congestion level is BUILDING, CONGESTED or DISSIPATING, or at least three vehicles are stationary together. Either lowers the severity to low and marks the stop 'queue member'. A lone stationary vehicle in free-flowing traffic is the obstruction candidate. The thresholds are untuned policy values."),
    ("Q79 [M] What is congestion hysteresis?", "A state machine FREE_FLOW -> BUILDING -> CONGESTED -> DISSIPATING that needs a confirming window before changing, so one noisy window cannot flip the state. Additionally an episode closes only after 60 s of free flow in the demo."),
    ("Q80 How is congestion measured?", "Per 10 s window: mean concurrency / an assumed capacity of 20 gives an occupancy proxy; the window is 'high' if mean speed <= 3 m/s or occupancy >= 0.6, 'low' if speed >= 8 m/s and occupancy < 0.6."),
    ("Q81 Why can a dense, moving road read as congested?", "Because occupancy >= 0.6 alone makes the window 'high' and 12 vehicles in a six-lane view is not slow. The incident discloses this; we did not retune it to fit a story. Per-carriageway capacity would fix it."),
    ("Q82 Why 60 seconds of close grace?", "The footage alternates between building, congested and dissipating about every 50-60 s; a 60 s grace merges these into 4 episodes instead of 13. It is a policy value tuned on one video, and ANVESH says so."),
    ("Q83 [M] How do you detect wrong-way driving?", "Per configured direction zone, take the track's net displacement while inside; if it is at least 60 px over at least 1.5 s and the cosine to the expected direction is <= -0.7 (more than about 134 degrees against), raise a high-severity candidate. A repeat close in time and space is treated as the same vehicle under a new ID."),
    ("Q84 Why is there no real wrong-way event in the main video?", "Nobody drives the wrong way in that footage. The rule is configured and unit-tested, and demonstrated on the SIMULATED camera, which shows the rule works but says nothing about its real-world accuracy."),
    ("Q85 [M] How do you detect overspeeding?", "Speed must be calibrated, inside the speed zone, above limit + 5 km/h with its lower error edge also above the limit, for 2 s continuously. The limit used (112.7 km/h = 70 mph) is a demonstration value because the real limit is unknown. Result tier is low."),
    ("Q86 [M] How is sudden braking detected?", "On 1.2 s regression speeds sampled every 0.25 s: starting speed >= 40 km/h, drop >= 25 km/h and larger than the error bands, deceleration >= 3.5 m/s^2 over 1.0-2.5 s, roughly monotonic fall, 30 s debounce. Earlier frame-differencing produced 199 false events in 160 s, so it was rewritten."),
    ("Q87 What is the aggressive-driving candidate?", "Three or more debounced lane changes within 10 s (a lane must be held 0.5 s). It is only a candidate indicator, never a finding of rash driving."),
    ("Q88 [M] How do you detect a collision?", "As an experimental candidate: two vehicles moving before contact, whose boxes overlap (IoU >= 0.10) for >= 1 s while closing, then both stop (<= 0.15 widths/s) within 2.5 s of contact for >= 2 s - and it is suppressed if >= 3 neighbours were already stopped (queue) or >= 60 % of other vehicles are stationary (standstill)."),
    ("Q89 [M] How do you avoid false accident detection?", "Box overlap alone never qualifies. Approach, sudden joint stop, and the queue and standstill gates must all pass; the simulated queue of eight stopped cars produces zero collisions. Still it is unvalidated - no real collision exists in the footage, so the false-positive rate in dense traffic is uncharacterised."),
    ("Q90 Did you train an accident model?", "No. No accessible, licensed accident dataset was available; the heuristic candidate is experimental and no accuracy is claimed."),
    ("Q91 What is the difference between a detected condition and a confirmed offence?", "A condition is what the detector saw; an offence is a legal finding. ANVESH produces candidates with uncertainty. A reviewer verifies the evidence and plate, an officer approves, and even then the challan is sandbox only."),
    ("Q92 Are red-light and illegal-turn detection available?", "Both detectors exist and are unit-tested, but need a configured stop line with a signal state source (the colour is not read from video) or turn rules. This footage has no signal or junction, so they are not applicable and not demonstrated."),
    ("Q93 What are the main false-positive risks of your detectors?", "Tracker identity switches and fragmentation, perspective in image-space directions, the extrapolated metric scale on the far carriageway, queue versus obstruction ambiguity, the congestion proxy and its assumed capacity of 20."),
    ("Q94 What is the post-incident disruption link?", "A congestion episode starting within 120 s after another incident in the same session is linked as 'temporally associated', with `causal_claim: false`. Proximity is not causation."),
]))

A(H2("18.7 Jam cause, plates, OCR"))
A(QA([
    ("Q95 How do you investigate the cause of a jam?", "Each window's evidence is checked against six hypotheses (bottleneck, stopped vehicle, collision-like obstruction, lane blockage, demand surge, signal restriction) and combined with Dempster-Shafer fusion into belief and plausibility. The ranking may return `insufficient_evidence` or `high_conflict`."),
    ("Q96 Why did the demo video produce no ranked cause?", "All four congestion incidents stored `insufficient_evidence` (H1 belief 0.0, plausibility 1.0). The footage has no obstruction or crash, there is one camera, and the load proxy alone cannot separate bottleneck from demand surge."),
    ("Q97 What is Dempster-Shafer and why not probabilities?", "A framework that assigns mass to sets of hypotheses and keeps the unassigned mass as ignorance, so 'we don't know' is not forced into a guess. It gives belief (lower bound) and plausibility (upper bound). ANVESH never prints a percentage confidence."),
    ("Q98 [M] What is ANPR?", "Automatic Number Plate Recognition: detect the plate in the image, crop it, run OCR to read the characters, and check the format. Registry verification is a separate step."),
    ("Q99 [M] Why is OCR not available?", "Two reasons. The footage is 640 x 360 and a plate is about 8 pixels wide - far too small. And OCR on saved incident evidence is not connected (the live plate reader added in Session 8 only fires on vehicles at least 110 px wide): the only plate dataset on hand is Vietnamese, there is no Indian training data, and OCR weight licences are unverified."),
    ("Q100 How can an officer still process a plate?", "They type it in from the evidence frame; a different reviewer verifies it. This is recorded as officer-entered / officer-verified, never as automatic recognition."),
    ("Q101 What is the difference between OCR confidence and registry verification?", "OCR confidence says how sure the reader is about the characters; registry verification says whether a vehicle with that plate exists. A confident wrong read of a non-existent plate would still fail the registry, and a registry hit does not make an OCR read correct."),
]))

A(H2("18.8 Evidence, audit, security"))
A(QA([
    ("Q102 [M] How is evidence stored?", "Key frames (unannotated originals), annotated frames and an H.264 clip are written once to the evidence folder under the incident id; their SHA-256, size and track id are recorded in an immutable database table."),
    ("Q103 [M] How do you prove evidence was not modified?", "Recompute the SHA-256 of the file and compare it with the stored value (the Verify action). The stored value cannot be updated (database trigger) and its creation is chained in the audit log. This shows modification, not who did it, and it is tamper-evident, not tamper-proof."),
    ("Q104 What happens if someone edits an evidence file?", "Verification reports a SHA-256 mismatch, the reviewer cannot pass the evidence step, and exported reports embed only files whose hash still matches."),
    ("Q105 What is the audit hash chain?", "Each audit row's hash is SHA-256 of the previous row's hash plus the row's content. Editing or deleting a row breaks all later hashes; 'Verify hash chain' re-derives them and reports the first bad row. Triggers also reject UPDATE/DELETE."),
    ("Q106 What happens when evidence is purged?", "Only the files are deleted, after a reviewed plan with a reason of >= 10 characters. The hash rows, a tombstone, the incident, case and audit remain, and the API answers 410 purged. Incidents with open cases are protected."),
    ("Q107 How are passwords stored?", "As scrypt hashes (N = 2^17, r = 8, p = 1) with a random 16-byte salt, from Python's `hashlib`. Plain passwords and hashes are never logged or returned."),
    ("Q108 What stops password guessing?", "Throttling: 5 failures per (user, address), 30 per address, 25 per user, each locking for 5 minutes. Unknown usernames behave identically. The counters are in memory, so they reset on restart."),
    ("Q109 What does the four-eyes rule do?", "The officer who approves must differ from the evidence verifier, the plate verifier and the case creator, and the plate verifier must differ from whoever entered the plate, so one person cannot push a case through."),
    ("Q110 Why is MFA missing?", "A TOTP secret must be stored in recoverable form, so it needs authenticated encryption with a key outside the database; no vetted cryptography library was approved for the pinned environment, and we did not want a fake MFA. A design is written down but not implemented."),
    ("Q111 What are the demo accounts?", "Accounts flagged `is_demo`, created with random passwords in `demo/data/initial_credentials.txt`. They cannot write operational data, and cannot create non-demo accounts."),
]))

A(H2("18.9 Backend, database, workflow"))
A(QA([
    ("Q112 [M] What is the role of the database?", "It is the system's durable memory: incidents, evidence hashes, plate readings, cases and transitions, registry lookups, users, sessions and the audit log. SQLite in WAL mode with triggers enforcing immutability and sandbox isolation."),
    ("Q113 [M] What happens if the server restarts?", "Everything stored survives (verified: 46 incidents and 2 cases unchanged, audit chain intact). A session that was running is marked `interrupted`. The live video and in-memory login counters are lost."),
    ("Q114 Why standard-library `http.server` and no web framework?", "To avoid adding dependencies to a pinned research environment and to keep the surface small. It is a ThreadingHTTPServer on localhost; a production deployment would sit behind a TLS reverse proxy."),
    ("Q115 How does the dashboard get live data - WebSocket?", "No. It polls: every 2.5 s on the live page and 4 s on lists, and the video is an MJPEG stream shown in an image tag. No WebSockets or Server-Sent Events."),
    ("Q116 [M] How does the officer workflow work?", "Open an incident, open a violation case, submit for evidence verification; a reviewer verifies the evidence; the plate is entered and verified by a second reviewer; the officer does a sandbox registry look-up, approves and submits to the sandbox simulator, giving a TEST-CHALLAN reference. Each step is audited."),
    ("Q117 [M] What happens if the registry is unavailable?", "The lookup returns not_connected / error / denied, never a guessed result. For an operational case approval stays blocked until a fresh authorised result exists; there is no override."),
    ("Q118 [M] Why is the challan simulated?", "No government e-challan API is available to this project; access is by departmental arrangement. The simulator exercises the workflow with clearly labelled TEST references so nothing real is sent."),
    ("Q119 Can a demo case become operational?", "No. A case's data class is fixed at creation (trigger), and sandbox lookups and hand-offs are rejected by the schema for operational cases."),
    ("Q120 How is a duplicate incident avoided?", "Each event has a natural key (session + the detector's own key such as the stop episode id). Re-sent events update the same row."),
]))

A(H2("18.10 Limits, future work, scaling"))
A(QA([
    ("Q121 [M] What are the main limitations?", "Detector accuracy on this footage is unmeasured; IDs fragment; speed is approximate; the congestion level is a proxy; wrong-way and collision are shown only on simulated sources; no plate OCR, real registry, real challan or MFA; unverified video licence; no real CCTV hardware test."),
    ("Q122 [M] What would you improve next?", "Label about 200 frames to measure detection and tracking and decide conf 0.10 / match 0.9 and the fine-tuned model; survey two distances for a defensible speed; per-carriageway congestion capacity; one queue incident instead of 14; then licensed higher-resolution footage for plates."),
    ("Q123 [M] How would this scale to 100 cameras?", "It does not today: one session per camera and one laptop GPU handling one 25 fps stream with limited headroom (about 1.4 streams by the 36 FPS estimate). A design would reduce per-camera fps (e.g. 5 fps), batch frames, use several servers, replace SQLite with a server database and the thread-per-request server with a production gateway. That is a design suggestion, not something built or tested."),
    ("Q124 [M] How would you deploy it in a traffic control room?", "Feed RTSP streams with credentials from the network team, calibrate each camera by survey, run behind a TLS proxy with secure cookies, add MFA/SSO, connect an authorised registry adapter, decide the AGPL and dataset licences, back up the data folder, and run a measured acceptance test per camera."),
    ("Q125 [M] How would you train the accident detection model?", "Collect licensed, labelled collision and near-miss clips from the real cameras, define the labels, split by incident (not by frame), train a detector or video classifier, evaluate on held-out incidents with recall and false alarms per hour, and keep the current gated heuristic as a baseline and a pre-filter. (The repository also has `docs/ANVESH_ACCIDENT_RESEARCH_PIPELINE.md`, which I did not review for this handbook.)"),
    ("Q126 [M] How would you improve the accuracy of speed measurement?", "Survey known distances (two or more) on the road, calibrate with a survey hold-out, use higher resolution and a camera aimed to keep vehicles larger, validate against a radar or reference vehicle, and then tune over-speed and braking on labelled examples."),
    ("Q127 Does it work at night or in rain?", "Not established. The training and the demo are daytime; no night or rain footage was evaluated."),
    ("Q128 Is the demo video legal to share?", "Its origin and licence are unverified (probably a YouTube download); it is treated as classroom-presentation only, git-ignored and never redistributed."),
    ("Q129 How do you know the system is not biased or unfair?", "Not established. Detection accuracy by vehicle type, time and place is unmeasured, which is exactly why the system outputs candidates for human review and never automatic penalties."),
    ("Q130 What was the hardest bug you fixed?", "The stop-episode lifecycle: an episode whose track vanished near the end of a window was closed in the next window with an end before that window's start, and was dropped, so 2 of 14 stops stayed 'still open' forever. It now reports the closed episode once; verified on the real footage (all 14 have an end)."),
]))

